>> title: Chāhan
>> category: Rice, Beans & Grains
>> tags: japanese, rice, egg, pork, leftovers, quick, weeknight
>> counters: Japanese Home Cooking
>> aka: チャーハン, chāhan, chahan, ちゃーはん, 炒飯, yakimeshi, 焼き飯, Japanese fried rice
>> pairs-with: gohan, chashu, sumashi-jiru
>> servings: 2
>> time: 15 min
>> slack: narrow — cold rice fries and warm rice steams, and a pan of steamed rice does not come back; the seasoning at the end has under a minute in it before the soy poured round the rim stops smelling of anything at all
>> step.1: The Japanese one, seasoned with soy, sake and white pepper. The site's egg-fried-rice is the Chinese version and is a different dish with oyster sauce in it; this is what a household does with yesterday's rice and the end of a piece of chashu.
>> step.2: scramble 20 sec, tip out loose
>> step.3: fry 3 min, pressing
>> step.4: everything back in, 1 min

The Japanese one, and it is seasoned differently: soy, sake and white pepper, no oyster sauce. The `egg-fried-rice` already on this site is the Chinese version and stays where it is. This is what a household does with yesterday's rice and the last of a piece of chashu.

Scramble @eggs{2} in @neutral oil{1%Tbs}(15 mL) in a #wok{} over the highest heat the burner has ~fry{20%sec}, and tip them out onto a plate while they are still loose and wet.

Fry @cooked rice{3%cups}(710 mL; cold, out of the fridge) with @chashu{4%oz}(115 g; in 1/4-inch dice) in the same pan ~fry{3%min}, pressing the rice flat against the metal and turning it, until the grains separate and clatter rather than clumping.

Return @&(2)scrambled egg{} to @&(~1)rice{} with @scallions{3}(sliced thin), @sake{1%Tbs}(15 mL), @fine sea salt{1/4%tsp}, @white pepper{1/4%tsp} and @soy sauce{1%Tbs}(15 mL) poured round the rim of the pan rather than onto the rice, and ~fry{1%min}. Soy that hits hot metal first smells toasted; soy poured on rice just makes it wet.
